\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Heller's free hypercube transformation}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Heller writes the fine site as #{x=2y+\xi},
with #{\xi\in\{0,1\}^4}, and uses Clifford
coefficient matrices to collect the complex
staggered fields into spin and flavor indices.
His forward transformation (3.4) has coefficient
#{1/8}, its inverse (3.5) has coefficient #{2},
and the right matrix factor in (3.6) is transposed;
see \citet{§3, pp. 3–4, (3.1)–(3.6)}{heller1997schrodinger}.}

\p{That section treats free staggered fermions.
The blocking needs an even number of fine sites
in each direction. In the Schrödinger-functional
time convention the final time-site label is odd,
while the total number of time slices is even.
The spatial size is even and carries the stated
twisted periodic conditions
\citet{p. 3 footnote and (2.6)}{heller1997schrodinger}.
Those boundary and normalization choices belong
to this transformation. They do not identify it
with the arbitrary-period cellular cochains used
in \ref{fdip-00VN}.}
